        "math_academy_id": "q-134653",
        "problem": "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2+x)^{8}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.87)^{8}.$ Give your answer to two decimal places.",
        "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+\u2026+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=8,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{8} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{0}){(2)}^{8}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{1}){(2)}^{8-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{2}){(2)}^{8-2}{(x)}^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =1{(2)}^{8}+8{(2)}^{7}{(x)}^{1}+28{(2)}^{6}{(x)}^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =256+8(128)(x)+28(64)({x}^{2})+\u22ef \\\\  & =256+1\\,024x+1\\,792{x}^{2}+\u22ef\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(2+x)}^{8}\u2248256+1\\,024x+1\\,792{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.13$ into the above approximation of $(2+x)^{8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.87)^{8} & =(2+(-0.13))^{8} \\\\  & \u2248256+1\\,024(-0.13)+1\\,792(-0.13)^{2} \\\\  & =256-1\\,024(0.13)+1\\,792(0.016\\,9) \\\\  & =256-133.12+30.284\\,8 \\\\  & =153.16,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto two decimal places.",
